abstract: >-
  Prior-data fitted networks (PFNs) learn from synthetic tasks, making their generalization critically dependent on the underlying pretraining prior. However, whether dynamically adapting this prior improves predictive performance under fixed computational budgets remains unclear.
  We introduce MAPLE, a framework for adaptive mathematical prior sampling guided by label-free disagreement between a PFN and a committee of classical regressors. MAPLE organizes {{ S.n_families }} mathematical function families into {{ S.n_cells }} task cells and employs a Kullback–Leibler-regularized sampler with theoretical guarantees of non-collapse, stability, finite-sample accuracy, and bounded prior distortion. A competence-based routing mechanism further combines PFN and expert predictions at inference.
  We evaluated MAPLE on {{ S.stats.real['128'].n_datasets_full }} real-world regression datasets across three training-set sizes and eight synthetic benchmark suites under matched pretraining budgets. Contrary to the central hypothesis, adaptive sampling did not improve over a uniform mathematical prior (pooled out-of-distribution difference: {{ pct(-S.stats.synthetic.RQ1_nrmse.pooled_ood.median_diff) }}; Holm-adjusted p = {{ num(S.stats.synthetic.RQ1_nrmse.pooled_ood.p_holm_family, 3) }}), primarily because disagreement scores provided insufficient separation between task cells. Although mathematical priors reduced synthetic out-of-distribution error by {{ pct(S.stats.synthetic.RQ4_synthetic_nrmse.ood_A_vs_P0.median_diff, 0) }} relative to a structural-causal-model prior, this improvement did not translate to real-world datasets.
  Competence routing consistently improved performance over the standalone PFN and outperformed tuned CatBoost with 128 training samples (Holm-adjusted p = {{ num(S.stats.real['128'].rq['RQ6_F_vs_CatBoost-T'].p_holm_family, 3) }}). At larger sample sizes, classical expert stacking achieved comparable or better performance.
  These findings establish theoretical and empirical boundaries for disagreement-guided prior adaptation and demonstrate that pretraining scale, prior complexity, and expert integration are more consequential than adaptive reweighting within the investigated computational regime.
keywords: [prior-data fitted networks, tabular regression, synthetic pretraining, adaptive sampling, expert disagreement, distributionally robust optimisation, mathematical priors, out-of-distribution generalisation]
